<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo VI the Wise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/866">866</a>–912</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Leo the Wise, Leo VI, Léōn ō Sophós · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo VI the Wise was Byzantine emperor from 886 to 912 and the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>, though his parentage is unclear: he was the son either of Michael III or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>. Reportedly very well read, he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilika">Basilika</a>, a Greek code in 60 books that translated and arranged the laws of Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, and issued new laws, the Novels, which removed obsolete Roman institutions such as the Senate and the consulate. His reign saw defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> at Boulgarophygon in 896 and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>, the last Byzantine outpost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, in 902. His fourth marriage, to Zoe Karbonopsina, caused a scandal; their son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise.jpg" alt="Leo VI the Wise" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Leo_VI_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leo was born on 19 September 866 to the empress Eudokia Ingerina, who was both the mistress of Michael III and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>. Basil assassinated Michael in 867 and became emperor, associated Leo on the throne in 870, and made him heir in 879 on the death of his older half-brother Constantine. Father and son disliked each other, and Basil almost had Leo blinded when he was accused of conspiring against him. Basil died in a hunting accident on 29 August 886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>One of Leo's first acts was to rebury Michael III with great ceremony in the Church of the Holy Apostles, which fed the suspicion that Leo believed himself Michael's son. He relied on bureaucrats such as Stylianos Zaoutzes and the eunuch Samonas, and his attempts to control great families such as the Doukai led to the revolt of Andronikos Doukas in 906. In December 886 he dismissed Patriarch Photios and replaced him with his own 19-year-old brother Stephen.</p><p>His fortune in war was mixed. He provoked a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> in 894 and was defeated, then lost the Battle of Boulgarophygon in 896 and had to pay annual tribute. He won a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in 900, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> fell in 902, the renegade Leo of Tripolis sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 904, and an expedition to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> failed in 911–912. The Rus' under Prince Oleg attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 907, and a trade treaty was signed after a second attack in 911.</p><p>He married four times. His first three wives died by 901, leaving no surviving heir, and he took Zoe Karbonopsina as mistress, marrying her after she bore a son in 905, against the opposition of Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos, whom he replaced with Euthymios. He had the boy, Constantine, crowned co-emperor on 15 May 908. Leo died on 11 May 912 and was succeeded by his brother Alexander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Leo wrote political orations, liturgical poems and theological treatises. His legal commission completed the codification begun under his father: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilika">Basilika</a>, in 60 books, arranged nearly all the laws of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>. His Novels then dealt with current problems and, with the Basilika, removed obsolete institutions such as the Curiae, the Senate and the Consulate from the law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-prefect">Book of the Eparch</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kletorologion">Kletorologion of Philotheos</a> were issued under his name, and he is the author or sponsor of the Tactica, a treatise on military operations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leo VI the Wise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The young Constantine VII, son of <b>Leo VI the Wise</b>, became nominal ruler of the Byzantine Empire under a regency council appointed by his dying uncle Alexander and headed by Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=895" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leo VI the Wise"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=895">What was here in 895 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
